{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1508040272,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMzNjY4NDg4NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1902#issuecomment-336684885"},"message":"or ideally we should start recording \"metadata version\", which would reflect the status of the parsers etc... not sure though if we could rely on ourselves to keep updating it manually whenever we update parsers etc","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1509273436,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM0MDI1MjQwMw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1902#issuecomment-340252403"},"message":"This issue touched upon at least three separate issues.\n\n- `--since` logic would be simple to add and useful: Aggregate subdatasets whose state in the super dataset has changed `--since`.\n- `--skip-aggregated` maybe be useful whenever one does not care about state/completeness of any existing aggregated metadata. For example, not caring that a subdataset may have changed its config since and now exposes more metadata (eg. more enabled parsers)\n- storing datalad version in the metadata (which part? dataset-global? each content metadata item?) would be of very limited use. The metadata content can easily change without any change in datalad itself, due to 3rd-party changes. For a complete picture, many more versions would need to be recorded, tests and acted upon for each individual dataset(-part)\n\nI can see myself implementing the first one, a see no real use case for the second one, as every time something updates a subdataset aggregation can be performed right away without any complicated retrospective decsision-making logic. Lastly, I don't think the 3rd one would lead to any practically usable benefits, but is guaranteed to bloat the metadata and the underlying code.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87